{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxkZASkxpVvRh4cNCXQJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four steps are done, and I checked the dashboard and the rule afterwards.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) uses `timestamp` as its time field. The response code is stored as text in `response` / `response.keyword`, not as a number.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created \"S4-6d23c3f5 Web logs\" with exactly two panels. One is a line chart of request count over time; the other is a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`, showing the top 20 codes.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created \"S4-6d23c3f5 5xx errors\" (id `f090e459-5d86-4eaf-aa85-99bc3ae2bdfd`) as an Elasticsearch query rule on the same index. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. A request matches when its response code is from 500 to 599. Because the code is stored as text, that range compares the codes as strings. This works for normal three-digit codes like 500 or 503. The rule was created enabled.\n4. **Edit and check:** The response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\", and the dashboard still has its two panels. When I read the rule back it showed enabled, with status \"ok\".\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":256,"cache_read_input_tokens":20930,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":256},"output_tokens":451,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da1e72d-e9d9-58d9-b388-fca8d0eba393","uuid":"bf878bc9-c466-4c93-98f9-b6e6dbc4c1cc","timestamp":"2026-10-12T17:39:20.275Z","request_id":"req_011CfxkZADd22xSgrL6rjjBU"}
